{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1691087199,"post_id":43565,"comment_id":93330,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s actually the opposite problem with his booster - he&#39;s so big that when we put him in the booster, his legs get caught on the bottom lip of the table. I&#39;ll add that to my question."},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user42851"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1691088233,"post_id":43565,"comment_id":93334,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Would a shorter booster work to let your son eat at the big table?"},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user42851"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1691088339,"post_id":43565,"comment_id":93335,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You could also teach your son some simple sign language, which kids his age can learn, so that he can say in sign &quot;I&#39;m done,&quot; or &#39;more.&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1691096554,"post_id":43566,"comment_id":93339,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This is exactly what I was thinking."},{"owner":{"account_id":4158318,"reputation":315,"user_id":9910,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/58YXd.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Mazura","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9910/mazura"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1691253093,"post_id":43570,"comment_id":93367,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;his <i>current</i> booster seat doesn&#39;t properly tuck in under our table and traps his legs&quot; +1"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1691286232,"post_id":43570,"comment_id":93372,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Please read the entire post before answering. While you&#39;re right that he&#39;ll probably figure out some other way to assert his agency, -1 for being quick to condemn something already explained in the body of the post."},{"owner":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1692092928,"post_id":43600,"comment_id":93464,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for sharing. How do you keep up with your friends with a baby on board?"},{"owner":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1692093058,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93465,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for sharing. It does sound tiring. Is it possible to hire a day-maid? In my country live-in maid is pretty common."},{"owner":{"account_id":9197749,"reputation":2046,"user_id":26398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SzmpQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pyrotechnical","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26398/pyrotechnical"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1692103490,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93477,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Juli yes, but such things usually cost money and that might not be viable if prior to kids, your friend was part of the household&#39;s income stream (i.e. going from 2 incomes to 1). Even if it is affordable, it doesn&#39;t fully negate the parental responsibilities, but it probably helps alleviate some of the pressure (i.e. it is so easy to fall behind on laundry)."},{"owner":{"account_id":9197749,"reputation":2046,"user_id":26398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SzmpQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pyrotechnical","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26398/pyrotechnical"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1692103732,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93478,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Juli one other thing to mention is that as parents it&#39;s soooo easy to fall off the radar of your friends. Before kids, I had a very active social life with a bunch of people that didn&#39;t have children. Afterwards, it&#39;s been nearly impossible to interact with those same people because they just don&#39;t understand where I&#39;m at with regards to my responsibilities. But truly, it is very helpful to not feel forgotten if as a friend you keep in touch via texting, it lets your friend respond when she has a few minutes."},{"owner":{"account_id":9197749,"reputation":2046,"user_id":26398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SzmpQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pyrotechnical","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26398/pyrotechnical"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1692103977,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93479,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Also, understand that if she were to call you she&#39;d either be too mentally exhausted to have much of a meaningful conversation or something (TM) would happen that would distract her. To exemplify, my mom called me over this past weekend at 2 in the afternoon, but the call happened right as I was walking in the door. So I tried to head downstairs to talk with her, but my son followed me because he just wanted some attention. He realized who I was talking to and wanted to be involved. Long story short, my mom had to download Duo, call back, my son showed her his bike, and then I had to go."},{"owner":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1692106634,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93481,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for enlightening me."},{"owner":{"account_id":18754944,"reputation":271,"user_id":44876,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/plmqA.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Kilobyte","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44876/kilobyte"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1692106649,"post_id":43600,"comment_id":93482,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What friends? ;) I generally fall off the radar for weeks or months, and then text or email them. I keep up the most with new friends that have babies near in age to mine because we&#39;re going through similar life experiences."},{"owner":{"account_id":18754944,"reputation":271,"user_id":44876,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/plmqA.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Kilobyte","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44876/kilobyte"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1692107159,"post_id":43600,"comment_id":93484,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"However, the companionship of old friends is also very important to me, because parenting has a way of swallowing my identity and interests. Old friends remind me that &quot;I&quot; am still here, somewhere... the me who always loved the outdoors, photography, writing and reading, camping... I&#39;m here somewhere, buried under diapers and onesies."},{"owner":{"account_id":6833993,"reputation":1782,"user_id":17701,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/171c374849c84ee6fa29f2130a0b4212?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"iulia","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17701/iulia"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1692384968,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93509,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Juli - to be fully honest it&#39;s hard having a meaningful conversation even with your partner in the same room, not to mention a phone call. For years on end, once you become a parent."},{"owner":{"account_id":9754817,"reputation":2492,"user_id":42067,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e8983c003003ba97dc6398b3be5b5bb3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R Davies","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42067/r-davies"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1692864275,"post_id":43627,"comment_id":93523,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"being presented with appropriately prepared finger foods also allows the child to practice their pincer grip when they pick up and manipulate food with their hands, as well as feel all sorts of different textures both with their hands and their mouth."},{"owner":{"account_id":4889488,"reputation":876,"user_id":39405,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/48248a8939c6db9b3d90f02b72354198?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"quarague","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39405/quarague"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":9754817,"reputation":2492,"user_id":42067,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e8983c003003ba97dc6398b3be5b5bb3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R Davies","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42067/r-davies"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1692864383,"post_id":43627,"comment_id":93524,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RDavies Excellent point, I will add that to my answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1693311061,"post_id":43631,"comment_id":93569,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;m so sorry to hear that! Weren&#39;t you invited to the birthday parties? My friend lives abroad since she&#39;s married, so it&#39;s impossible to keep a touch except through texting."},{"owner":{"account_id":17329492,"reputation":397,"user_id":39952,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d6b22b27acaa28d8b29cfbbfb38340b9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"sisee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39952/sisee"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":29222655,"reputation":149,"user_id":44874,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5d7a61c88f8ad008074f421ec3247ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Juli","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44874/juli"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1693400660,"post_id":43631,"comment_id":93575,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"She did not celebrate her own birthday - these were birthday parties for the children. Or she simply had other children overnight and was busy with them."},{"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1693739239,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93612,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Pyrotechnical make your life easier: check the fridge then give your kids 2 to 3 available options for lunch :-)"},{"owner":{"account_id":9197749,"reputation":2046,"user_id":26398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SzmpQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pyrotechnical","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26398/pyrotechnical"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1693944196,"post_id":43606,"comment_id":93623,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Ivana bold of you to assume I have that much mental energy. A lot of times I&#39;m going off of things like seeing cheese in there in the morning, perhaps not realizing that it was only 1 slice or that it got used for something else. Bear in mind, for this example, I wasn&#39;t anticipating coming up with options, it occurred after asking the kids 3 times what they want and moving a couch; none of which was part of the original plan which might&#39;ve included having them help me assemble a lunch."},{"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1694423417,"post_id":43631,"comment_id":93645,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I feel this answer. But remember @sisee if she answered an email after months she did think about you and she took the hurdle of returning an email after months which is not easy."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1695087482,"post_id":43689,"comment_id":93710,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Interesting, though I don&#39;t understand it either. I can understand vinegar removing calcium and magnesium from the diaper, but not how the accumulation of the minerals would cause ammonia smell."},{"owner":{"account_id":1703956,"reputation":103,"user_id":7350,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SU0xt.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Danny Beckett","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7350/danny-beckett"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1695099474,"post_id":43690,"comment_id":93714,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You can buy water softener if your water is hard"},{"owner":{"account_id":492960,"reputation":1129,"user_id":31889,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d0ed96380268ce904eb69d0895915cfa?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"jpa","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31889/jpa"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1695104426,"post_id":43689,"comment_id":93716,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Could be something similar to uric scale, which builds up in toilets in hard water areas. In any case, acidic wash sounds like worth trying."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1703956,"reputation":103,"user_id":7350,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SU0xt.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Danny Beckett","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7350/danny-beckett"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1695107154,"post_id":43690,"comment_id":93718,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@DannyBeckett I don&#39;t think water softeners are good for people or the planet. There are actually medical benefits to hard water. If the problem lies in the diapers, better to soften the water where it&#39;s needed. If scale is clogging pipes, that&#39;s another story."},{"owner":{"account_id":6883407,"reputation":124,"user_id":35915,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/xFRFY.png?s=256","display_name":"WaterMolecule","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35915/watermolecule"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1695140344,"post_id":43689,"comment_id":93729,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"As mentioned by @j4nd3r53n, vinegar lowers the pH (which in clearer terms just means adding more H+ to the solution), which will convert some ammonia (NH3) to odorless aqueous ammonium (NH4+) or ammonium salts (like ammonium acetate, NH4+ CH3COO–)."},{"owner":{"account_id":7310275,"reputation":101,"user_id":42449,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/76215b61d2db3f176b702b4b23397ca0?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"D Duck","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42449/d-duck"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1695161020,"post_id":43690,"comment_id":93735,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Borax has been banned in the EU and the UK"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":7310275,"reputation":101,"user_id":42449,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/76215b61d2db3f176b702b4b23397ca0?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"D Duck","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42449/d-duck"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1695169069,"post_id":43690,"comment_id":93736,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@DDuck - I didn&#39;t know that! Thanks for letting me know. I can&#39;t comment on vinegar because I have no experience with it. The part about washing with <i>very hot water</i> shouldn&#39;t pose a problem, though."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1695199900,"post_id":43689,"comment_id":93740,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Depending on your machine, using a food acid in powder form might be easier.  Citric acid (home brewing, preserving) and tartaric acid (baking) are quite readily available. I use citric acid for descaling sometimes"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1695213115,"post_id":43697,"comment_id":93747,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I know another reason why the vinegar rinse helped. Is the diapers were boiled in soapy water, especially if the water was hard, the soap and the minerals in hard water form a kind of scum, which is broken down with weak acids. That&#39;s why hard water deposits on shower walls, etc., are so well removed by an acid (my choice is citric acid, common in lemons,etc.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":9267126,"reputation":141,"user_id":44342,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b6d4bdfec6939b15f35c41030c4c8305?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Lehue","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44342/lehue"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1695275970,"post_id":43697,"comment_id":93754,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I always put white vinegar in the pre-wash and in the fabric softener tray and wash at 60&#176;C. This works like a charm. Also since I put vinegar in the fabric softener tray, jeans that used to smell after drying don&#39;t smell anymore. So just replace fabric softener with vinegar."},{"owner":{"account_id":18754944,"reputation":271,"user_id":44876,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/plmqA.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Kilobyte","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44876/kilobyte"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1695390552,"post_id":43697,"comment_id":93790,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I have seriously considered switching to cotton prefolds for this very reason. Boiling sounds like such a simple fix! I strongly suspect my pocket diapers comprised of bamboo/microfiber inserts and polyester/PUL covers couldn&#39;t handle it. I would readily switch to cotton prefolds and I have them lying around, they&#39;re just a lot harder to get on a wriggling baby in my experience. And she wriggles a lot! Maybe there&#39;s a knack to it I&#39;m missing."},{"owner":{"account_id":9267126,"reputation":141,"user_id":44342,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b6d4bdfec6939b15f35c41030c4c8305?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Lehue","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44342/lehue"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1695799381,"post_id":43697,"comment_id":93816,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;m using cotton prefolds from the start and my baby is now 17 months old. What helps if she wriggles too much is giving her something to occupy her while putting on the diaper (e.g. a toy, a sock, anything that&#39;s lying around and might be interessting). If the baby wants to stand up close the cover in the widest option, place the prefold inside, let her step in and tighten the cover around her."},{"owner":{"account_id":2966345,"reputation":1797,"user_id":31475,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Wmix3.png?s=256","display_name":"Martijn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31475/martijn"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1697963946,"post_id":43769,"comment_id":93922,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I did not actually measure it, but I dont think it was far off of 14C (exceptional cold wind, from a cold winddirection which doesnt occur often). I&#39;ll go check the suit if there is more info. He was definitally cold though, cold arms/legs/nose/ear."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2966345,"reputation":1797,"user_id":31475,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Wmix3.png?s=256","display_name":"Martijn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31475/martijn"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1697965948,"post_id":43769,"comment_id":93923,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Martijn Cold hands are not necessarily a reason to worry, they can occur even if the child is perfectly comfortable. You get a better estimate when you touch the back of the neck or the rump."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2966345,"reputation":1797,"user_id":31475,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Wmix3.png?s=256","display_name":"Martijn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31475/martijn"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1698014844,"post_id":43769,"comment_id":93925,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Martijn - One of my grandchildren was in the NICU for a while, so their parents were quite worried about taking her home. Their Neonatologist told them, &quot;Cold hands, warm feet&quot; was the goal. So, cool hands with the child in a sleep sac was not a problem at all. Legs shouldn&#39;t be cold."},{"owner":{"account_id":9754817,"reputation":2492,"user_id":42067,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e8983c003003ba97dc6398b3be5b5bb3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R Davies","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42067/r-davies"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1698047665,"post_id":43769,"comment_id":93927,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"We have m boy in a cold bedroom, at that age he was in a 3.5tog baby sleep sack(with arms out), long onesie and a vest. We also had a thermometer in there so we could tell the temperature."},{"owner":{"account_id":2966345,"reputation":1797,"user_id":31475,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Wmix3.png?s=256","display_name":"Martijn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31475/martijn"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1709066189,"post_id":44020,"comment_id":94439,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"He cant move yet, we&#39;re working on that (as far as you can work on that). They daycare he goes to is otherwise in line with what we like, so we prefer to keep this. In march he will go to another (wednesdays), we will see how that goes but we&#39;re expecting the same outcome thb."},{"owner":{"account_id":2610902,"reputation":121,"user_id":45541,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3KEGdMcl.png?s=256","display_name":"Cincinnatus","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45541/cincinnatus"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1709207293,"post_id":43937,"comment_id":94451,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I ween thou mayst mean &quot;wean&quot; :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1709627188,"post_id":44038,"comment_id":94499,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Welcome to having young children. They continuously develop and what worked yesterday may not be good enough today. Prepare yourself for lots of rounds of trying things out."},{"owner":{"account_id":4680966,"reputation":101,"user_id":45707,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/14a35cf9e580008611f9781b01135f27?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"FlyingTeller","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45707/flyingteller"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1709795084,"post_id":44038,"comment_id":94523,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"For us, the last point was also a game changer. We had several weeks when, according to our pediatrician, our kids where mentally able to want things that are further away from them and recognize the whole room but weren&#39;t able to get to anything. Those were also highly care intensive and then it just suddenly puffed away. Have encountered several more episodes like that, always prior to or accompanying major development steps"},{"owner":{"account_id":2966345,"reputation":1797,"user_id":31475,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Wmix3.png?s=256","display_name":"Martijn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31475/martijn"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1712133582,"post_id":44020,"comment_id":94660,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You answer has a part of what turned out to be the issue: He was extremely frustrated that he couldnt do anything. He can now move around and that alone reduced it 80%."},{"owner":{"account_id":4889488,"reputation":876,"user_id":39405,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/48248a8939c6db9b3d90f02b72354198?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"quarague","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39405/quarague"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1719218278,"post_id":44198,"comment_id":94834,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I would also consider the timing of the pre-bedtime meal. If dinner is say 2 hours before bed-time plus 10 hours of sleep, then 12 hours without food is a long time for a 14-months old. Maybe add another snack just before bed-time."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1722444218,"post_id":44272,"comment_id":95066,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What&#39;s a marketeer?"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1727456156,"post_id":44020,"comment_id":95216,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;...it&#39;s SUPER boring to sit in one spot all day and not do anything!&quot; You called it perfectly. You often do."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":5,"creation_date":1735168597,"post_id":44460,"comment_id":95494,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I agree with you, there is no authoritative literature on whether or not to swaddle. But wow, is it wonderful! The position the American Academy of Pediatrics takes is, literally, &quot;The AAP recommends that parents swaddle their baby if they would like to.&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":958948,"reputation":119,"user_id":54456,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/45beee9b157c1cace13d6fdf180af9df?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Dennis Jaheruddin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/54456/dennis-jaheruddin"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1735409330,"post_id":44460,"comment_id":95495,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It may not be considered controversial depending on where you live. As far as I can see it is well embraced in the US and less so in Europe. Every site I check says &#39;this is how you can minimize risks&#39; so though there are benefits there are clearly risks as well."},{"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1735423320,"post_id":44460,"comment_id":95496,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There is essentially no risk if you use common sense and take the time to read basic guidelines. But websites will talk about minimizing risk because, unfortunately, many parents have no common sense and will do dumb things unless you give them explicit instruction not to. Just keep your baby in a bassinet, lie them on their back, and be careful when they start to roll."},{"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1735424029,"post_id":44460,"comment_id":95497,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Going without a swaddle can be done as well, but I believe what you&#39;ll find is that occasionally your baby will startle and flail their arms, which will wake them up. With a swaddle this is less likely to happen and your baby will sleep more soundly. I&#39;d be curious to get @anongoodnurse&#39;s thoughts on that."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":958948,"reputation":119,"user_id":54456,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/45beee9b157c1cace13d6fdf180af9df?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Dennis Jaheruddin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/54456/dennis-jaheruddin"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1735432644,"post_id":44460,"comment_id":95500,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@DennisJaheruddin - Any site claiming it reduces risk (of SIDS) is incorrect. There&#39;s no evidence of it influencing SIDS rates at all. Nor is there evidence that when done as per common-sense guidelines it poses any risk to the baby."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1735433020,"post_id":44460,"comment_id":95501,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Cdn_Dev - The infant startle response (aka Moro reflex) does, indeed, disturb the infant&#39;s sleep. The infant was used to being enclosed in the womb, which dampens the Moro reflex. What a shock it must be to be completely free, and sometimes such shocks aren&#39;t a wonderful thing. I&#39;m very much in favor of swaddling. Agree 100% that those who use it read about it first. People can do incredibly dumb things (some of which keep ER doctors in business). (Don&#39;t get me wrong, I&#39;ve done the same dumb things <i>that I treated</i> myself! D&#39;uh!)"},{"owner":{"account_id":38622961,"reputation":11,"user_id":54294,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a79073fe93b4f0af21109f07f70845f6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Pachafish","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/54294/pachafish"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1736243074,"post_id":44473,"comment_id":95537,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for your answer! Did you have any difficulties leaving the room before she fell asleep? My husband is very reluctant to leave the room because he says she cries in distress when he does. I’m not so sure it is entirely distress."},{"owner":{"account_id":39028062,"reputation":1,"user_id":55013,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0c947d0a1f3d33939b76e01686184e86?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Steph","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/55013/steph"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":38622961,"reputation":11,"user_id":54294,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a79073fe93b4f0af21109f07f70845f6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Pachafish","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/54294/pachafish"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1736352682,"post_id":44473,"comment_id":95539,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi Pacha, around that age yes we did have difficulty. But we made ourselves leave as long as she had everything she needed (nightlight (Hatch), pacifier, Squishmallow). We would let her cry until she calmed down, which might take a few minutes. How long does he wait after he leaves?"},{"owner":{"account_id":39267904,"reputation":41,"user_id":55479,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7405809dba4fd51166a310dc2cd333c7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Charles L","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/55479/charles-l"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1736870540,"post_id":44482,"comment_id":95551,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Love the Plan idea. I m definitely going to try that :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1736888185,"post_id":44482,"comment_id":95553,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"-1 for &quot;[too] young to see a psychiatrist.&quot; Please provide a source (yes, it&#39;s your opinion, but why? Is that a good approach?) Who (in the community) are going to be best able to recommend &quot;a rock star therapist&quot;? (The psychiatrist or a very knowledgeable primary care physician.) Who is going to prescribe the therapist (an MD. Psychiatrists are MDs. Otherwise it&#39;s likely out-of-pocket.) Additionally, a child psychiatrist will be better able to diagnose (or order the appropriate testing for) a potential neuropsychiatric disgnosis, as well as consider medical causes for excessive anxiety."},{"owner":{"account_id":15143298,"reputation":1257,"user_id":45689,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/43000f52d05ea2e6867c8356cf9040b6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"curiousGal","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45689/curiousgal"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1736936851,"post_id":44482,"comment_id":95555,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse you likely have access to medical sources that show the long term benefits of psychiatric evaluations for five-year-olds, and we will be interested to read your references.  As this is a parenting forum and not a medical forum I am relying on my own parenting experiences.  My experience was that it was worth it to pay out of pocket for a great therapist, recommended by friends, neighbors, community members, rather than rely on whom my HMO sent me to."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1736956762,"post_id":44482,"comment_id":95557,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What is emotional therapy? In the US, it&#39;s an adult option, usually for couples. You recommend it, but don&#39;t explain what it is or the benefits of it for a child. Also, from your edit, you mention anxiety as a sign of high intelligence. Do you have a source for this?"},{"owner":{"account_id":15143298,"reputation":1257,"user_id":45689,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/43000f52d05ea2e6867c8356cf9040b6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"curiousGal","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45689/curiousgal"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1737006699,"post_id":44482,"comment_id":95560,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse I think in the US you call it psychotherapy.  The correlation between intelligence and anxiety is well known.  Here is one source: <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3269637/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3269637</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":40218966,"reputation":129,"user_id":56954,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2780ed588cab9597598ee550436c91d5?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Ask Pir","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/56954/ask-pir"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1739843528,"post_id":44531,"comment_id":95646,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"With her current car seat, she is in a five point harness, and like I said even with the infant insert she still has a ton of room to grow into it. The main thing is we need to make sure that she stays at the Newborn recline, and is given the proper support that her current bucket seat gives her. She does need to stay rear facing for a long time as well. When it comes to helping her climb up into her car seat, she would need help climbing up onto the floorboard, and from there up onto the vehicle seat, then up and over the side of the bucket seat. So she would need three separate aids to help"},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1751251260,"post_id":44690,"comment_id":95993,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;Kids have basically no capacity for deception until around 3-4 years old&quot; Babies learn to fake crying to get attention much younger than that."},{"owner":{"account_id":5126259,"reputation":355,"user_id":19888,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7d7b72e342b5e235ff60fdd88ae2697a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Ettina Kitten","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/19888/ettina-kitten"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1751758922,"post_id":44690,"comment_id":95999,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@nick012000 On what basis are you calling that crying fake? Children cry for many reasons, and loneliness and separation anxiety are among those reasons, so crying for attention could still be a genuine expression of distress."}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":260}